<!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"cs-inner-lead"} --><p class="cs-inner-lead">Silicon Geek exists to make students more capable, not more dependent. Our rule is short and clear: <strong>Learn first. Build honestly. Cite properly. Submit only work that you can explain and defend as your own.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"cs-callout cs-callout--tip"} --><div class="wp-block-group cs-callout cs-callout--tip"><!-- wp:heading {"level":3} --><h3 class="wp-block-heading">The self-check</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Before submitting, ask: “Can I draw the block diagram, explain the main circuit/code flow, say why each key component was chosen, describe my test results and failures, and answer basic viva questions without reading someone else’s script?” If the answer is no, the work is not yet ready as your own.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">1. Purpose of this policy</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This Academic Integrity Policy explains how Silicon Geek’s free learning material and personalised guidance may be used responsibly for assignments, laboratories, mini/major projects, reports, presentations, examinations and career portfolios. It protects students from plagiarism risk, protects institutions’ trust, and protects the credibility of honest learners.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This policy does not replace your university, college, school, examination-board or employer rules. If there is any conflict, follow the stricter applicable rule and ask your teacher/guide for clarification before submitting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">2. What academic integrity means</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:list --><ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Your submitted work honestly represents your own learning, effort and contribution.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Ideas, text, diagrams, data, code, designs and libraries created by others are credited.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Test results are real, traceable and not altered to appear better than they are.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Help received is used for understanding and is disclosed where the institution requires it.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>During an individual/closed assessment, you use only the resources expressly permitted.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">3. Permitted use of Silicon Geek</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Students are welcome to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list {"className":"cs-checklist"} --><ul class="wp-block-list cs-checklist"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Study notes, diagrams, solved PYQs and learning paths for concepts and revision.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use project guides to understand a block diagram, component choice, interface, algorithm and test method.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Adapt a learning example for a different application after understanding, changing, testing and documenting their own decisions.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Ask for hints, explanation, review of their own draft/circuit/code, debugging direction and viva practice.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use report/PPT structure suggestions while writing in their own words from their own observations.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Reuse properly licensed open-source code or standard interfaces with attribution and licence compliance.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Show failed attempts and limitations honestly; real troubleshooting is valuable engineering evidence.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">4. Prohibited conduct</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Silicon Geek does not support:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list --><ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Submitting notes, reports, presentations, circuit designs or code copied from Silicon Geek or elsewhere as if entirely self-created.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Asking any person/service to secretly complete a graded assignment, laboratory record, project report or portfolio item in your place.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Using guidance, messaging, remote access or another person during a live examination, viva, practical test or proctored assessment where unauthorised.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Fabricating observations, readings, graphs, screenshots, survey responses, bibliography entries or completion dates.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Removing another author’s name, watermark, copyright notice or licence information.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Paraphrasing copied content only to evade plagiarism or AI-detection tools.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Submitting the same substantially identical work for multiple courses without required permission.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Sharing restricted question papers, institutional logins or confidential material.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">5. Citation and attribution checklist</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For every external idea or asset used in a report, presentation or codebase, record:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list --><ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Author/organisation and title of the book, paper, datasheet, web page, repository or video.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>URL/DOI/version and access date for online sources where your format requires it.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Exact figure/table/code portion reused or adapted, and what you changed.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Library/framework name, author, version and licence.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Quotations marked as quotations; summaries written in your own words and cited.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Help received from teachers, peers, laboratories or services where acknowledgment is required.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A bibliography alone does not make copied paragraphs original. Cite at the point of use and make your own analysis, selection, calculation and explanation visible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">6. Responsible use of AI and online tools</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>AI tools, code generators, circuit simulators, grammar tools and search engines may be used only where your institution permits them and only as learning support. You are responsible for every formula, connection, statement and line of code you submit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list --><ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Ask for explanation and test the suggestion; never submit an unverified AI-generated circuit/code/result.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Check dimensions, units, component limits, pin names and signs against textbooks and datasheets.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Never upload confidential, personal or restricted institutional material to a tool without permission.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Disclose AI assistance when your institution requires disclosure.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Do not generate fake observations, fake references or text intended only to bypass integrity checks.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">7. Group projects</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In a group project, every member should have a real, recorded contribution. Keep a task list that identifies who researched, designed, wired, coded, tested, documented and presented each part. Rotating explanation practice is recommended so that one member does not become the only person able to answer viva questions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a guide outsourced the core learning task, do not hide that concern until evaluation day. Use available time to study the implementation, retest it, document actual results and inform your project guide if institutional rules require disclosure.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">8. Evidence of honest practical work</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Keep an engineering trail as you build:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list --><ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Dated block-diagram and circuit revisions.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Component calculation and selection notes.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Build photos before and after wiring.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Raw readings, failed tests, debugging hypotheses and corrected faults.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Code versions with meaningful comments and changed sections.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Observed result, limitation, safety precautions and future improvement.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This trail improves viva confidence and makes it easier for a teacher to see genuine learning, even when the first version did not work.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">9. What happens if a request is not allowed?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Silicon Geek may decline or stop a request that involves cheating, impersonation, fabricated evidence, plagiarism or another violation. Where possible, we will explain the boundary and offer an ethical alternative—for example, converting “make my report” into “review my outline,” “teach me this code,” “help me debug my actual output,” or “practice likely viva questions from my implementation.”</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Students remain responsible for any institutional consequence of submitting work that violates their institution’s rules. If you are unsure whether a type of help is permitted, ask your faculty/guide before using it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">10. Our commitment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:list {"className":"cs-checklist"} --><ul class="wp-block-list cs-checklist"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>We create explanations that teach reasoning, not memorised submission text.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>We show limitations, safety, troubleshooting and sources, not only ideal results.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>We do not advertise guaranteed marks, ranks, approvals or placements.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>We correct identified errors and respect third-party intellectual property and licences.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>We coach students to explain their own work with confidence and honesty.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->

